WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.110
CARTER MORGAN: Kubernetes
is amazingly powerful.

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:03.660
But if you're just
getting started,

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.850
it might also seem
amazingly complicated.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.763
So let's learn how
to debug applications

00:00:07.763 --> 00:00:08.680
running on Kubernetes.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.440
Hi, Kaslin.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.402
Thanks for coming by.

00:00:11.402 --> 00:00:13.110
I've been learning a
lot about Kubernetes

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.079
from you, about pods and
services, containers and more.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:18.360
And they mostly
work pretty well.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.250
But what do I do when
things aren't working?

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.500
KASLIN FIELDS: Oh,
that's a great question.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.950
Learning how to debug Kubernetes
is very useful, especially

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.250
because applications on
Kubernetes can grow so large,

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.050
it can be very
difficult to know what's

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.360
going on in your system
at any given time.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.320
CARTER MORGAN: Exactly.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.030
If I was just doing
everything manually

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.470
and only had a
few instances, I'd

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.720
know how and where to look
for the information I need.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.370
But with Kubernetes
abstracting away

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.120
the infrastructure
behind the application,

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.560
I don't even know
where to start.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.852
KASLIN FIELDS: Well, let's
start with what you do know.

00:00:46.852 --> 00:00:48.642
How do you currently
debug Kubernetes?

00:00:48.642 --> 00:00:50.475
CARTER MORGAN: The
kubectl command line tool

00:00:50.475 --> 00:00:52.560
is some useful tooling
I've been using.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.860
I found them using
kubectl --help,

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:57.810
but my favorites so far
are kubectl explain,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:01.560
kubectl describe,
kubectl logs, and kubectl

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.480
get with the -o flag.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.980
KASLIN FIELDS: How
do you use those?

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.397
CARTER MORGAN:
Well, I use kubectl

00:01:06.397 --> 00:01:08.430
explain whenever I come
across a new API object

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.480
that I don't know.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.950
What's especially
nice about it is

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.480
how I can dig into
the documentation

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.950
of specific fields, too.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.740
So it's like a one-stop command
line shop for defining objects.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.500
Kubectl logs is
useful because it

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.750
lets me see what's
happening inside of a pod.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.670
It logs events from
containers and stores

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.410
them so I can
easily access them.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.260
Then I use the trusty kubectl
describe to look at pod states

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:28.950
and events.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.450
KASLIN FIELDS:
That's a great start.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.030
Kubectl describe
can tell you a lot.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.220
For instance, if
a pod is pending,

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.650
that could mean you have
insufficient resources that

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.590
prevent the pod from getting
scheduled onto a node.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.142
CARTER MORGAN: So
maybe in that case,

00:01:42.142 --> 00:01:44.100
I could delete another
pod or create a new node

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:44.952
to make space.

00:01:44.952 --> 00:01:47.410
KASLIN FIELDS: Yeah, that might
be a good way to handle it.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:50.640
Another state a pod can be
in is the waiting state.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.140
CARTER MORGAN: Oh,
I have seen that.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.515
KASLIN FIELDS:
Yeah, that happens

00:01:53.515 --> 00:01:56.670
when a pod has been scheduled
onto a node, but it can't run.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.310
For instance, maybe the
image can't be pulled.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.350
So even though there's
space to run the app,

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.718
it just won't work.

00:02:02.718 --> 00:02:03.510
CARTER MORGAN: Hmm.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.030
What about other cases, like
if there's space to run the app

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:09.370
and the image is working,
but the app itself is broken?

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.130
I'm not sure how to debug that.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.600
KASLIN FIELDS: For
cases like that,

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.220
kubectl exec is your friend.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.040
CARTER MORGAN: Oh,
let me look that up.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.670
Kubectl exec lets me execute
a command in a container

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.520
or even open an interactive
shell in the container.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.765
Wow, that's useful.

00:02:24.765 --> 00:02:26.640
KASLIN FIELDS: So if
you needed to dig around

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.410
inside of your
application, maybe

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.210
to see where files
are being stored

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.000
or what ports are accessible
inside the container,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.533
exec is a really
useful tool for that.

00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:36.450
CARTER MORGAN: Mm, OK.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:39.150
So to summarize, depending
on the state my pod is

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.710
in-- whether it's
pending, waiting,

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.780
or otherwise running
but somehow failing--

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.880
that determines what
my issue might be.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.150
KASLIN FIELDS: Yes, and by
using logs, the exec command,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.310
and other very
simple commands, you

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.320
can generally find
out enough information

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.930
to troubleshoot
your application.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:55.680
CARTER MORGAN: Wow,
thanks again to Kaslin

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.020
for always dropping knowledge.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.520
In this episode, we learned a
lot about debugging Kubernetes

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:00.362
application.

00:03:00.362 --> 00:03:02.070
For more information,
check out the links

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.160
in the description below.

